speechtext:
While we are very anxious to do all we can to meet the views of hon. gentlemen opposite, we have to consider what is going to be most effective in the long run in meeting the wishes, not of hon. gentlemen opposite, .but of the veterans, and we are taking a course which we believe will lead to that end.
This measure has been introduced amongst other things to give relief to the man of forty-five, fifty or sixty years of age, who is prematurely aged. Instead of having to wait until seventy years of age, as he would under the Old Age Pension Act, the returned soldier will receive the equivalent of the old age pension at the, age of sixty-five. Or if declared "burnt out," as it is expressed, he may receive this equivalent at a much earlier age, at any age in fact. My hon. friend says we are putting him in the class of paupers, and that the Old Age Pension Act is a paupers' act. That is something entirely new;
I have never before heard the old age pension measure interpreted as an act of charity on the part of the government.
